          correct: 2,
          explain: 'The detection of molecular hydrogen (H2) in Enceladus\'s plume by Cassini\'s Ion and Neutral Mass Spectrometer (INMS) (Waite et al. 2017, Science) is considered one of the most significant astrobiological discoveries of the Cassini mission because of what it implies about the chemistry occurring at the ocean floor. Serpentinisation is a specific exothermic chemical reaction between liquid water and iron- and magnesium-rich silicate minerals (particularly olivine and pyroxene): Mg2SiO4 (forsterite) + H2O → Mg3Si2O5(OH)4 (serpentine) + Mg(OH)2 (brucite) + H2. The reaction is highly exothermic (releases heat), requires water to be in contact with fresh, unreacted silicate rock, and produces molecular hydrogen as a by-product. On Earth, serpentinisation occurs at slow-spreading mid-ocean ridges and at the Lost City Hydrothermal Field in the central Atlantic, where water penetrates into fresh peridotite and reacts at 40–90°C, producing H2 and alkaline fluids. The H2-rich fluids support dense ecosystems of hydrogen-consuming bacteria and archaea (methanogens: CO2 + 4H2 → CH4 + 2H2O) — demonstrating that H2 as an electron donor can power life without sunlight. The detection of H2 in Enceladus\'s plume (along with CO2, CH4, and silica nanoparticles — which indicate water that cooled from >90°C) implies that the same serpentinisation reaction is occurring at the interface between Enceladus\'s liquid water ocean and its rocky core, maintaining elevated temperatures and producing a chemical energy gradient (H2 + CO2 → CH4 + H2O, a reaction that can power methanogenesis) that could sustain microbial life entirely independent of sunlight. This makes Enceladus one of the most compelling astrobiological targets in the solar system. Answer A is incorrect — H2 does not indicate a pristine solar nebula composition; serpentinisation actively alters the water chemistry from primordial. Answer B incorrectly proposes biological nitrogen fixation — H2 is produced abiotically by serpentinisation and is not a nitrogen fixation by-product. Answer D incorrectly proposes a pressure-depth relationship for H2 concentration — H2 is a chemical reaction product, not a pressure tracer.',
